  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 222 PM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nUpper heights will rise briefly tonight behind today's shortwave\ndeparting through the Great Lakes and the next shortwave coming\nin late Friday. The Friday night/Saturday shortwave will quickly\nmove off into northern MN and then Canada, leaving southwesterly\nflow aloft again for Sunday and into the work week. A lead\nshortwave coming in Monday will help push a decent surface low\nand baroclinic zone into the Plains, although exact track is\nstill uncertain. The main upper trough pushing through late\nMonday into early Tuesday, with high pressure building in behind\nit. Near zonal flow sets up for mid-week with a few weak\nshortwaves possible near the Canadian border. \n\n...Fog tonight and Friday morning...\n\nSatellite loop shows the edge of the stratus deck approaching\nour far western counties, and could see some clearing in that\narea this evening. However, fog will quickly fill in and several\nof the CAMs show a fairly dense band from the Cooperstown area\ninto the northern Red River Valley. A few of the models have the\nworst of the fog further west, and exactly where the line\nbetween fog and low clouds will be is highly uncertain. HREF has\nhighest probabilities for less than a mile or even half a mile \nin visibility along and west of the Red River Valley, so kept\nthe areas of fog mention there and patchy fog everywhere else.\nHow many areas will be under a quarter of a mile is still \nuncertain, so will keep out any headlines for now. Will likely \nneed a dense fog advisory somewhere in the region overnight. \n\n...Rain Friday night and again Monday/Tuesday...\n\nAnother quick moving shortwave coming through Friday night into\nSaturday morning will bring another round of light rain to the\nregion. Probabilities of a tenth to a quarter of an inch of rain\nis over 60 percent, but drops off quickly for over half an inch.\nSome weak instability of less than 500 J/kg of MU CAPE could\nbring a few claps of thunder, but nothing else impactful. A\nstronger system moving in Monday into Tuesday could bring a more\ninteresting setup, with some ensemble members showing portions\nof our forecast area in the warm sector with machine learning\nhaving a low probability for severe. Others have the track of\nthe low a little further south and more stratiform precip\nsetting up. While chances of severe are not high, there is over\na 70 percent chance for an inch of rain or more over portions \nof the forecast area, so another round of soaking rain seems\nlikely and will continue to message.",
